bianbu-agent-security (0.1.41) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Replace the flat read/write path lists with include + exclude ranges. A
    scope is now `{include: [...], exclude: [...]}`; excludes must sit inside
    an include entry so a dead declaration cannot be saved. An excluded read
    path becomes fully invisible (`InaccessiblePaths`), an excluded write path
    stays readable but loses write access (`ReadOnlyPaths`).
  * Drop the custom JSON group. Permission settings are only the structured
    read, write and network scopes now, so a preset or policy reset has nothing
    left to leak around.
  * Derive the execution boundary instead of storing it. `securityd` decides
    host or sandbox from the settings an Agent declares: any read, write,
    network or syscall declaration needs a namespace, so it runs sandboxed;
    nothing declared runs on the host. A missing trusted manifest still forces
    the sandbox. An Agent can no longer name a side.
  * Remove the syscall denylist baseline. `SECCOMP_DENYLIST` is empty, no entry
    is mandatory, and the seccomp builder returns no policy file when the
    effective denylist is empty so `--seccomp` is skipped entirely. Additions
    are the only source of the filter.
  * Stop replacing tool policies on re-registration. The client exposes
    `get_capabilities` and the Runtime only registers rows securityd has not
    seen, so an operator's tightening survives every restart.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:40:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.40) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Default every tool policy to allow. The catalog shipped with `ask` as the
    built-in default, so a fresh install prompted for almost every tool. Every
    catalog entry, the unmapped fallback for tools outside the catalog and the
    capability registry fallback for unregistered tools now resolve to
    `allow`. The registry delegates to `capabilities.unmapped_policy()` so the
    two defaults cannot drift apart again.
  * Split the syscall denylist into its own WebUI page. It is a kernel seccomp
    table rather than a filesystem or network scope, so it gets a dedicated
    tab with its own agent and architecture selectors; the capability page
    keeps the readable, writable, network and custom groups.
  * Default the new-policy select and the tool-catalog hint to `allow`.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:25:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.39) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Record the framework declared by a signed package. A package registers
    through `install_package` / `register_capabilities`, and that path never
    stored which framework's tool catalog the Agent is wired to, so the console
    fell back to listing every known tool: `com.bianbu.runtime` reported the
    runtime and hermes catalogs mixed together (81 tools) while a logical
    Agent, which registers through `register_agent`, reported the correct 34.
    The optional `[agent].framework` value is now parsed (an unknown value is
    rejected instead of silently scoping to the wrong catalog), passed through
    `record_manifest`, and stored in `agent_manifest`. A package that does not
    declare it keeps whatever a caller already registered, so an upgrade cannot
    blank the scope.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:10:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.38) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Decode the D-Bus permission payload before validating it.
    `SetPermissionGroup` takes its value as an `sss` string because D-Bus
    cannot carry an arbitrary JSON value, and the client encodes arrays and
    objects as JSON. The service forwarded that string straight to
    `permissions.normalise`, so every `read`/`write` save failed with "read
    需要是路径数组" and the control plane could not store a permission range at
    all. The payload is decoded in the service layer now, and a payload that is
    not valid JSON is rejected before it reaches the engine.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:05:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.37) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Add missing columns when upgrading an existing database. The manifest
    refresh failed with `sqlite3.OperationalError: no such column: framework`,
    so the Runtime refused to start after the upgrade. `CREATE TABLE IF NOT
    EXISTS` keeps an existing table as it is and never adds a column, and the
    tool-schema migration only rebuilt `capability` and `session_trust`; the
    `framework` column added to `agent_manifest` was left out. A column-level
    migration now runs before the others and registers every column appended
    after the first release, so an upgraded database reaches the same shape as
    a fresh one.
  * Resolve the tool catalog from the client's own install root.
    `capability_catalog` imported `securityd.capabilities` with a bare import,
    but callers only publish the `client` directory on `sys.path`; the
    `securityd` package sits next to it, so the import failed and the function
    silently returned an empty catalog. The console then fell back to the
    per-Agent capabilities and both frameworks rendered the same rows, which
    defeats the framework-scoped catalog.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.36) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Replace the capability model with a three-state tool policy. A capability
    used to carry four dimensions (domain, risk level, confirm strategy and
    scope) that mostly restated what the tool name already implies. The policy
    is now one dimension, the tool itself, with three states: allow, ask and
    deny; the old ``capability``, ``scope`` and ``risk_level`` fields are gone
    from the decision path and from the audit payload.
  * Split the old scope into five structured permission groups: readable
    range, writable range, network access, a syscall denylist and a custom
    JSON group. ``permissions.py`` owns the groups and the engine aggregates
    network and syscall on top of the read/write/custom core.
  * Give DLP the same three states plus ``mask``, with a single
    ``DEFAULT_ACTION = mask`` for every unset PII type, so a policy never has
    to encode a per-type risk column.
  * Keep the tool catalog per framework: a runtime agent is offered the
    Runtime tools and a hermes agent the hermes tools, and an unknown tool
    falls back to ``ask`` instead of a framework-wide default.
  * Migrate the capability database in place. The legacy schema has no
    ``tool`` column, so a plain ``CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`` kept the old
    table and every insert failed with ``no column named tool``; the registry
    drops and rebuilds ``capability`` and ``session_trust`` when it detects
    the old layout.
  * Remove the token budget feature end to end: the meter, the D-Bus getters
    and setters, the runtime token guard and the runtime security consumer are
    all gone.

 -- liudecheng[bot] <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.34) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Make the `last_seen` column of the Agent page match the real activity time.
    Two problems made it look wrong: the manifest installation time was used as
    a fallback evidence, so an Agent that was installed but never ran appeared
    as "recently active"; and `installed_at` was returned as the raw SQLite UTC
    string while `last_seen` was ISO-8601 with an offset, so a renderer that
    localized it shifted the value by one time zone. `last_seen` now merges
    only real activity evidence (audit chain, syscall audit log, caller
    binding) and stays empty when there is none, so the console can say "no
    activity" instead of showing an installation timestamp. `installed_at` is
    normalized to ISO-8601 UTC milliseconds like every other timestamp.

 -- liudecheng <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:10:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.33) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Fill in `last_seen` in `ListAgents` so the Agent page of the security
    console stops showing an empty "recent activity" column. The engine merges
    the newest evidence from the audit chain, the syscall audit log, the caller
    binding table and the manifest installation time, and normalizes every
    timestamp to ISO-8601 UTC milliseconds. Agents that own capability rows but
    no manifest no longer render the literal "None" for version, status and
    installation time. New unit tests pin the four evidence sources and the
    audit-chain shard backtracking.

 -- liudecheng <decheng.liu@spacemit.com>  Wed, 23 Sep 2026 11:50:00 +0800

bianbu-agent-security (0.1.32) resolute-porting; urgency=medium

  * Fix launch-lease expiry to use the monotonic deadline recorded at issue
    time instead of the wall clock. The first NTP sync on a cold-started K3
    steps the wall clock forward (measured 1281.5s), which expired a 30s lease
    issued only 1.4s earlier and blocked Agent Worker startup with
    `lease 已过期`. Leases issued in a previous boot are treated as expired;
    rows written before this change fall back to the wall clock. The atomic
    one-shot consume in commit() and engine.sandbox_policy() share the same
    predicate.

